update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('db4dc1df-5fab-4cd9-b137-b0d1cb3ef2c2'::uuid,'aa23d2dc7138ef03f1debf9daafb5eb2','"Usually not instinctual" is a claim about how organisms with elaborate brains actually behave. The stimulus gives a requirement for flexible behavior, so it can only tell us who can''t behave flexibly. An organism with plenty of neurons could still run on instinct most of the time without breaking any statement.'),
('fde33acb-dc48-4914-ae5d-4ea0b6e14c71'::uuid,'c106d3c01b1f4e31bd497874f5fbad99','Run the chain from insects. No insect brain provides a sufficiently large number of neurons, and flexible behavior requires one, so no insect brain is capable of flexible behavior. The stimulus defines flexible as noninstinctual, so whatever insects do is instinctual.

"Exclusively" sounds strong, but it''s earned: the requirement is missing for every insect, so there is no room for even a little flexible behavior. When a strong word shows up on Must Be True, check whether a universal rule (here, "no insect brain") backs it before crossing it off.'),
('06296952-c65d-422b-814b-1714860093e6'::uuid,'b87904bb0419e9d44d1b66bac8d90a15','Larger than an insect''s brain isn''t the same as large enough. The stimulus only says insect brains fall short. A brain could beat an insect''s and still lack the neurons. And even a brain with enough neurons isn''t guaranteed flexible behavior, because the neuron count is a requirement, not a guarantee.'),
('a804f0e2-9d72-492b-9f50-465b9ca0598b'::uuid,'a738854f62018292603520cd1eea31cf','This reverses the rule. Capable of flexible behavior → large number of neurons says the neurons are required. D reads it as large brain → equipped for flexible behavior, treating the requirement as enough. Driving legally requires a license, but holding a license doesn''t make someone a driver. D also swaps "large brain" for "large number of neurons," which the stimulus never equates.'),
('364af33b-7f64-43bb-86c8-340bfd18bdc1'::uuid,'42b75ffe4b1127a15f32646c53134d8a','E says purely instinctual → brain of insect size or smaller. We only know the other direction: insect → instinctual. An organism with a bigger brain that still falls short of the neuron count would also be limited to instinct, and nothing rules that out. "Only" makes a small brain a requirement for pure instinct, and the stimulus never sets that requirement.'),
('056ef2d1-49b7-4b02-9413-37c76abe8f04'::uuid,'7c6f201bc777c7e136e120aa94019118','This is the contrapositive we built. Suppose Lind is in and Knight is also in. Knight drags Jones in, and now the book has all three, which rule 2 forbids. So Lind in means Knight out.

The move to repeat: when one rule forces an item to bring a second, check whether that pair runs into a limit (here, "not all three") that blocks a third. That blocked combination is often the inference.'),
('3da72e13-099e-4e71-8017-9ce07d568803'::uuid,'ed314d846335c68a1e203a5aa65560b7','Only one author? Build a legal book with two: Knight and Jones. Rule 1 is met, rule 2 is met because Lind is out, and Knight brought Jones as rule 3 demands. Two authors and no broken rule, so B doesn''t have to be true.'),
('bfd9faf1-42e7-437a-8694-114278eaf426'::uuid,'edb19383c949514b98bccb3bb00a4d85','Knight can be in. A book with Knight and Jones and no Lind satisfies every rule. The rules only say that Knight''s presence has consequences (Jones in, Lind out). They never exclude Knight.'),
('4aea2107-e33a-476a-8976-9d0a8ff528dd'::uuid,'8d964525edaaf41407e4b0b4da305de9','Lind doesn''t bring Jones. A book with Lind alone satisfies all three rules: at least one author, not all three, and rule 3 never triggers because Knight is absent. So L → J doesn''t have to hold. D borrows Jones from the Knight rule and attaches it to Lind.'),
('505588b9-5225-42bd-b00f-be2dd5519c9d'::uuid,'3d5bb50911c444dd8f1bbd807e61c5a8','Lind isn''t required. A book with Knight and Jones satisfies every rule without Lind, and so does a book with Jones alone. Rule 1 says at least one of the three is in, not which one.'),
('f911fdd7-afab-4167-af27-26f26553d414'::uuid,'8d5f2d0d11f068832e58f398208f3441','A starts from people who do not undergo mediation. That negates the trigger of the first rule, so it tells us nothing. And to reach "should be sure," A has to assume these people should litigate. Nothing says that someone who skips mediation should go to court. They could drop the conflict entirely.'),
('bff43783-8559-4be8-87d1-ed77f11a82ca'::uuid,'e4e5d49dbddc90119c06fa664552d1a6','Not based on ideology negates the trigger of the third rule, so we learn nothing about compromise. Even if we did, no rule ever says anyone should pursue litigation. The litigation rule only gives a requirement (being sure), never a recommendation.'),
('e4f4654f-7376-4704-8718-90428d405d04'::uuid,'c35a955777515e0e0e39b8f6d82b00c8','C connects ideology to being sure. The rules link ideology to compromise, and compromise to mediation. Being sure only appears in the litigation rule, which connects to nothing else. People in ideological conflicts could be completely sure of themselves without breaking any rule.'),
('080c7abc-5921-4fd1-b619-c4b96d25c39e'::uuid,'97bb71fb72d216b55b90b950fff30513','Being sure is the necessary side of the litigation rule, so it can''t trigger anything. D also ties sureness to mediation, but sureness never touches compromise. Someone sure of their position and willing to compromise could undergo mediation, and no rule says otherwise.'),
('a280a233-6f6c-4923-b295-fee7b5205c3e'::uuid,'62cef43d1edbb227819344e620bf9af9','Chain the third rule into the contrapositive of the first. I → ~WC, and ~WC → ~M. So I → ~M: people whose conflicts are based on ideology are not people who should undergo mediation. That''s E.

The move: find the term two rules share (willingness to compromise), line the rules up so the shared term sits in the middle, flipping one with its contrapositive if needed, and read off the two ends.'),
('0f5087ba-df7c-499e-bf24-5b56650c1294'::uuid,'27b3dd5cdc7947223e8c1be8610ed9d3','This is the chain read end to end, in the same hypothetical mood as the stimulus. Listed → easier to limit → many people limit → their health improves. "Many people" guarantees "at least some people," so the weaker wording is safely proven.

Notice A says "would" and "if exact caffeine content were listed." It doesn''t claim anyone''s health is improving now, which matters because the first sentence tells us the content isn''t listed. A good Must Be True answer keeps both the chain and its conditions.'),
('ceecc249-0dfe-43e7-81fa-a32d74805625'::uuid,'a1f19720da4ca4a30ae93ecbd2f5cc6a','B starts from the actual situation: content not listed. That negates the trigger of the second statement, and a negated trigger proves nothing. The stimulus says listing would make limiting easier. It never says people can''t limit at all without it. B turns "easier" into "possible."'),
('b88a2b33-fa8d-4d1e-b886-b2d24a79f699'::uuid,'90ad818fa815e8b4b94900f7517a8fce','C also starts from content not being listed (people have to guess), and then switches from limiting to eliminating. The stimulus is explicit that listing would help people limit, "but not eliminate," their intake. It says nothing about how hard eliminating is when people have to guess.'),
('46dd0aeb-c5da-48cd-aa91-fa90a4831a7d'::uuid,'5c1033db9fbd855320925645026f9dfe','The only health effect the stimulus describes is improvement for the many people who limit their intake. Could someone else''s health get worse? Maybe, but nothing in the statements says so, and a Must Be True answer needs proof, not possibility.'),
('9d203d1a-6832-4fce-bcb2-7c0b8cc90f70'::uuid,'5613dc5f733ad54c7522f8a60fa73921','↓TC → ↑C runs the chain backward. Lower congestion sits at the far right: it''s what higher profits require. A requirement doesn''t trigger anything. Congestion could fall while the cost of living and the number of residents stay exactly where they are. The valid version goes the other way: ↑C → ↓TC.'),
('e338d583-4982-45f7-9c6c-b5716cdd2329'::uuid,'b8fdb2ac3bfd5fe54cd36d708648de56','Start at the left end. A lower cost of living guarantees more consumers living downtown (second statement), and more consumers guarantees higher profits (first statement). Two links, so ↓CL → ↑P.

The move on a chain question: find the term that is the result of one rule and the trigger of another, connect them, then check each answer by asking whether its "if" sits to the left of its "then" on your chain.'),
('341e2b59-68f5-4027-83a0-0018d9606aa2'::uuid,'49467b64dbeb8579e18037c1872ff766','C starts from the right end of the chain, which triggers nothing, and lands on an increase in the cost of living, which no rule even mentions. The statements only cover what follows a decrease in the cost of living. Nothing could prove the cost goes up.'),
('7dbb893a-33e7-4a2b-898b-627e3bb7fc00'::uuid,'0ce122e75f4751ffc12da90ccc9f8a2e','D is the full chain read in reverse. The statements give ↓CL → ↓TC: if the cost of living falls, congestion must fall too. D flips it to ↓TC → ↓CL. Lower congestion is only a requirement for higher profits, so it can''t send us back up the chain.

This is the most tempting trap because the two terms really are linked. Only the direction is wrong.'),
('972db7df-6686-420e-b5a9-11dd101ad4c9'::uuid,'da66f04d00d736c89ffeaf8aa0cddb15','The first statement says more consumers → higher profits. E reverses it: higher profits → more consumers. Profits could rise for some other reason, like more shoppers commuting in, and the statements never say residents are the only way to raise profits.'),
('f017809c-eba1-4690-b9ee-e7604fa70181'::uuid,'2975704debba45378b89107646d912cf','D substitutes one term for its partner. Claim 2 pairs each speed doubling with a cost doubling. Claim 1 says speed doubled because transistors doubled, so each transistor doubling is a speed doubling. Put transistors where speed was, and you get D. "Fabricating" is just another word for producing, and D keeps the mid-1990s window.

When two statements share a term, try substituting one into the other. On many Must Be True questions, that substitution is the whole inference.'),
('2d2fe406-e5ce-4583-840d-a52161e420b4'::uuid,'51505aa016acb0a55db60ab5a1acefda','A reverses the rule. Extreme difficulty is required for addictiveness, not enough for it. A also shrinks "most users" to a single person. One person struggling to quit can''t satisfy a requirement about most users, and even satisfying it wouldn''t prove the substance is addictive.'),
('90fbac33-61e9-4516-997e-67dbee871ef4'::uuid,'e91eaeea96115ff93dc2e169ce2a825a','The expert compares an adequate definition with a rejected proposal, not with present practice. We have no count of what is deemed addictive now, so we can''t prove the number would shrink.'),
('c4dc1647-96ad-4328-9d6a-b8b4a0bdbb98'::uuid,'a715deae110602931a42149f98c1d45c','Let''s unpack C. Its subject is a substance some users can quit easily. It says that substance is addictive only if most users can''t quit easily:

Addictive → NOT (most users quit with little or no difficulty)

The expert''s rule gives Addictive → most users have extreme difficulty. If most users have extreme difficulty, it can''t be true that most quit with little or no difficulty. So C follows.

The "some users" opening is what makes C look hard, but it''s only setup. A few easy quitters don''t decide anything, because the rule cares about most. When an answer wraps a rule in extra conditions, strip it to its arrow and compare it with the stimulus''s arrow.'),
('97165d62-07e7-4c1c-8861-fd500ca40145'::uuid,'0460d227e6f214fb26d0239a89a5f560','D talks about one person who used a substance without difficulty. The rule is about withdrawal causing difficulty for most users. Easy use says nothing about quitting, and one person says nothing about most. To conclude nonaddictive, we''d need the requirement to fail for most users, and D never shows that.'),
('de07c050-44d2-4d44-bdb8-13e62d7a2a29'::uuid,'38f9a97b2f65103933ffc32aab6397b8','The expert says what any adequate set of criteria must embody, which treats adequate criteria as possible. Disagreement over one proposed test doesn''t show the term can''t be defined precisely. Nothing supports "impossible."'),
('c75aa3a7-3401-497d-b02b-b4c882f18cb5'::uuid,'cbd2ab91c3bc4373651b978c378c42aa','A never says the word comes from an older work. If the correction fixes an obvious typo in a quotation from a modern work, rule 3 lets the editor correct it without explanation. And even in an older work, a word that occurs only once isn''t frequent, so rule 2, the only rule that calls for supplying an explanation, never triggers.'),
('c33e1704-8443-498b-8e31-4a8f638c1816'::uuid,'6df871ffe63d69ff3a1a0362ada26679','Rule 3 is the only rule about modern works, and it covers obvious typographical errors, not archaic spellings. Rules 1 and 2 cover older works only. Nothing in the manual permits modernizing an archaic spelling in a modern work, with or without an explanation.'),
('91e7e837-4afa-4412-aaf0-3f25a6ee17a7'::uuid,'757018dba439b8a4ee06f12a417907f4','The style manual never says an editor should modernize anything. Rule 2 gives permission ("may") when archaic forms occur frequently. Interference with comprehension only tells us rule 1''s preserve requirement doesn''t apply. Escaping a duty to preserve isn''t the same as gaining a duty to modernize.'),
('4222be4e-c90c-4c88-9bae-76474b680a0b'::uuid,'84ba9297cb6e2f8596869152b0d0650c','D gives us punctuation quoted from an older work that occurs frequently. That''s the full trigger for rule 2, so the editor may modernize it. The extra detail, that it interferes with comprehension, doesn''t hurt: a rule still applies when you know more than its trigger. And D keeps the manual''s "may" instead of upgrading it to "should."

On rule-application questions, check two things: is the whole trigger present, and does the answer keep the rule''s strength?'),
('226569f5-8de0-449f-9c0c-3084624242ac'::uuid,'352f366262eccb6d1511d0b17b8c61b4','Rule 2 allows a general statement in the preface when similar modernizing has been done in more than one quotation. E describes modernizing only one instance, so the preface option isn''t triggered. E also turns the manual''s options into a "should": the manual says where an explanation can go, not that it must go in the preface.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;